      "problem": "$39\\text{ mod }4=$",
      "worked_solution": "The residue of $39$ modulo $4$ is the integer $0\\le r<4$ such that\n\n$$\n39≡r\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $39$ as an integer multiple of $4,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $39≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$ because\n\n$$\n39=9\\cdot 4+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $39$ modulo $4$ is $3.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the subtraction property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}39 & ≡39-0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡39-9\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡39-36 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $39$ modulo $4$ is $3.$",
